// resolve_actor.dg — the single source of truth for "who is really acting," used by the small
// set of Deluge functions that still need it after the client-side CRUD split (see
// ../actions/README below and ../../CLAUDE.md): the three Sales-Manager-only actions
// (dispatch/redispatch/supervision-batch, which change deal ownership and need a real
// server-side profile gate) and the compensation-email sender.
//
// zoho.loginuserid is NOT client-supplied — it comes from Deluge's own session, so a malicious
// widget can't fake it — but it's the user's EMAIL, not their CRM record id (see
// get_user_id_by_email.dg's header for why). This resolves that email to a real user id, and
// applies the ONE allowed impersonation exception: the test/admin viewer (Hassen, see
// ../../app/js/viewAs.js's DEV_VIEWER_ID) may pass viewAsUserId to test as another profile — and
// it only takes effect when the REAL session really is his, never based on anything the client
// claims about itself.
//
// Calling another Standalone function from inside a Deluge function requires the qualified
// `standalone.<name>(...)` form, not the bare name — confirmed live (calling `getUserIdByEmail`
// bare deployed but resolved to nothing at runtime until qualified). Every call from one of this
// repo's own functions into another follows this same convention.

string standalone.resolveActor(String viewAsUserId)
{
	HASSEN_EMAIL = "hassen.b@docshipper.com";
	realEmail = zoho.loginuserid;
	realUserId = standalone.getUserIdByEmail(realEmail);

	if (realEmail == HASSEN_EMAIL && viewAsUserId != null && viewAsUserId != "") {
		return viewAsUserId;
	}
	return realUserId;
}
